A||||1||BN Boomer||87 vs 88 2.5s revisited
Z||000000||BN Boomer||08-25-99||09:00 AM||||Well, I finally bothered to pull the cradle out of my 87 SC and remove the engine. What a messy pain in the a$$. Anyhow, this car has an automatic transmission and the engine that SCCA FIERO gave me is out of an 88 with a manual transmission and also has the force balancer. I removed the flywheel from the 88 and installed the flex plate and shim from the 87. Here's where things got a little dicey. The clearance between the crank snout of the 88 engine and the bolts retaining the 87 flex plate so small that it does not permit the use of a socket, hence I am unable to properly torque the bolts. I tightened the bolts as much as I could with an open end wrench. <p>My questions are, are there differences between the 87 and 88 2.5 crankshaft snouts, do I need an 88 flex plate, are there differences between engines meant to be installed in automatics and those meant for manuals, any other input?<p>Thanks,<br>Boomer<br>
Z||000001||theogre||08-25-99||10:10 AM||||don't forget to remove the pilot bushing. The front of the TC wants that space empty.<p>A thinwall socket won't fit them? that's odd. Don't think I've ever heard of that.<p>pull the TC out of the tranny and make sure it sits flat on the flext plate. the center nose on the TC should drop into the pilot bushing well in the crank tail. the bolt points should all contact the flex plate. If this all happens, then there is no diferance you need to worry about. <b>DO NOT use the bolts to draw the TC down.... If it doesn't line right up it is not going to. You WILL bend the flex plate if the bolt pads do not rest freely on the flex plate.</b><p>I don't believe that the flex plate on the 4's provides any balancing for the crank..... One thin you could do is look up both years flex plates in the parts books and see if they are the same.... I think they are.<p>don't forget to dump out all the old oil in the TC, and blow out the cooling lines before you put things back to gether. Pull the pan off the tranny dump the oil, clean the magnet, and replace the filter. You'll need around 7 quarts of tranny fluid to refill. (poor in 3 quarts then start the car and work in the rest till it fills.)
